Subject: [PATCH 12/12] Project-Drive renderer: --tumble (spin the car in the
 air) and per-step tyre telemetry
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Two additions to the CPU reference renderer, both aimed at the XPBD soft-tyre blow-up:

* --tumble / --tumble-at / --tumble-rate / --launch
  Body rates are injected in world axes at a chosen time (default 4.30 s, where the
  scripted drive leaves the ramp at 11.26 m/s). --launch adds an impulse to buy airtime.
  New --mode close: a body-relative three-quarter close-up so the wheels are actually
  visible. --skip-before renders only a time window without shortening the simulation.

* --tyrelog FILE
  240 Hz per-wheel dump straight out of XPBDSoftTyre: contact node count, max contact
  lambda, deepest penetration, all five SoftTyreResidual families (spoke / shear / hoop /
  lateral / diagonal), rim arrival and residual, TyreReaction Fx/Fy/Fz, inflation force,
  gauge pressure, hub Z, wheel omega, patch centre — 77 columns.

What the dump shows on a landing: 407 nodes in contact, 0.156 m penetration, the rim-flange
constraint handed 0.159 m, and a reaction of 998 kN through ONE wheel in a single step —
313x the static wheel load — while the per-node contact lambda stays at 0.0016. With
h = 1/(240*8) s that is ~5.9 kN per node from lambda/h^2, summed over ~400 nodes with no
clamp: the mechanism behind XPBDSoftTyre.cpp's unclamped lambda/h^2 sum, now measured
instead of read out of the source. The control run (identical drive, no spin) still peaks
at 29.7 kN = 9.3x, so the spike is not an artefact of the tumble.

Also fixes a corrupt CSV header: the column-name fprintf passed 19 varargs to 18
specifiers, so the last column of every wheel was garbled and readers silently read
shifted fields. The header is now written in a loop.
